   "subject": "Re: [TMB users] NA/NaN Function evaluation, seemingly from dnorm",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The Laplace approximation that is used for intergration does not work well with a mixture prior on the latent variable (needs to be Gaussian).<div><br></div><div>If you want to do this in TMB you are probably better off using numerical quadrature:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/namespaceromberg.html#a980bb141c963b1cf20da450063c5c7d1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/namespaceromberg.html%23a980bb141c963b1cf20da450063c5c7d1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462237347000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3wAfBSaHKSaX6eGdwHZkdX\">http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/namespaceromberg.html#a980bb141c963b1cf20da450063c5c7d1</a><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "smktS_fjAgAJ",
   "from": "Josh",
   "author": "402856652ba9dda3",
   "date": "2020-03-02T19:26:37Z",
   "subject": "Re: [TMB users] NA/NaN Function evaluation, seemingly from dnorm",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;m aware of that, but as mentioned above that doesn&#39;t appear to be what is causing the issue here.  The issue instead appears to arise from the call to <span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">dnorm</span> <span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34)\">for</span> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:monospace;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">x.</span></span></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Monday, March 2, 2020 at 2:08:31 AM UTC-8, Hans Skaug wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">The Laplace approximation that is used for intergration does not work well with a mixture prior on the latent variable (needs to be Gaussian).<div><br></div><div>If you want to do this in TMB you are probably better off using numerical quadrature:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/namespaceromberg.html#a980bb141c963b1cf20da450063c5c7d1\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/namespaceromberg.html%23a980bb141c963b1cf20da450063c5c7d1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462237347000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3wAfBSaHKSaX6eGdwHZkdX\">http://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/namespaceromberg.html#a980bb141c963b1cf20da450063c5c7d1</a><br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "miv3E3ZWAgAJ",
   "from": "Kasper Kristensen",
   "author": "258b98968d0ad8b9",
   "date": "2020-03-12T15:17:54Z",
   "subject": "Re: [TMB users] NA/NaN Function evaluation, seemingly from dnorm",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I did not try your example, but I see a potential numerical issue here:</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>res(i) = 0;<br>for (int j = 0; j &lt; means.size(); j++)<br>{<br>  res(i) += prop(j)*dnorm(lw(i), means(j), sigma(j), false);<br>}<br><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">You might consider doing the accumulation in log-space:<br><br>logres(i) = -INFINITY;</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br>for (int j = 0; j &lt; means.size(); j++) {<br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
